"A friend says, \"Blue always makes people calm.\" What would you say?": "08b6eefc2268",
"Artists also call the hues from red through yellow warm, and the hues from blue-green through blue-purple cool. People have used these words since at least the late 1700s, but they do not agree on exactly where the line falls. You may also hear that warm colors excite people and cool colors calm them. Researchers who reviewed the studies say it is too early to make strong claims like that. The rules for matching colors are guides. They are not laws, and tastes differ from person to person and from culture to culture. Use the wheel to find ideas, and then trust what you see.": "56ddfcec7e4f",
"Black letters on white paper have a strong contrast.": "2d7e1d19d32e",
"Colors on opposite sides of the wheel are called complementary colors. On the painter's wheel the pairs are red and green, yellow and purple, and blue and orange. Side by side they give a strong contrast. The French chemist Michel Eugène Chevreul studied this at the Gobelins tapestry works in Paris, where people had complained about the black dye. He found that the dye was good. The black only looked weak and reddish when deep blues surrounded it. A color, he wrote, seems to shift toward the opposite of its neighbor. He published his findings in 1839.": "b71a3b984eca",
"Colors that sit next to each other on the wheel are called analogous colors. Red, red-orange, and orange are one example. Neighbors have a lot in common. A picture that uses an analogous color scheme looks like one family of color, with little contrast between its parts.": "f614ca4a4157",
"Cut two small squares from the same sheet of gray paper. Lay one on black paper and the other on white paper. Now they do not look the same. In 1810 the German writer Goethe noted that a gray shape on a black background looks much brighter than the same shape on white. Nothing happened to the paper. Your eyes and brain judge every color against the colors around it. This effect is called simultaneous contrast, and it is why artists think about colors in groups.": "c07288af35e4",
"Cut two small squares from the same sheet of gray paper. Lay one on black paper and the other on white paper. Now they do not look the same. In 1810 the German writer Goethe noted that a gray shape on a black background looks much brighter than the same shape on white. Nothing happened to the paper. Your eyes and brain judge every color against the colors around it. This effect is called simultaneous contrast, and it is why artists think about colors in groups. To plan those groups, artists bend the colors into a circle called a color wheel. Each place on the wheel is a hue, such as red, orange, or blue. Isaac Newton put the first color wheel in his book Opticks in 1704. In 1766 the English artist Moses Harris wrote about a wheel built on red, yellow, and blue, with orange, green, and purple between them. Painters still use a wheel like that one. Screens use another wheel, built on red, green, and blue. Colors that sit next to each other on the wheel are called analogous colors. Red, red-orange, and orange are one example. Neighbors have a lot in common. A picture that uses an analogous color scheme looks like one family of color, with little contrast between its parts. Colors on opposite sides of the wheel are called complementary colors. On the painter's wheel the pairs are red and green, yellow and purple, and blue and orange. Side by side they give a strong contrast. The French chemist Michel Eugène Chevreul studied this at the Gobelins tapestry works in Paris, where people had complained about the black dye. He found that the dye was good. The black only looked weak and reddish when deep blues surrounded it. A color, he wrote, seems to shift toward the opposite of its neighbor. He published his findings in 1839. Which color is the opposite of red? It depends on the wheel. On the painter's wheel the answer is green. On the screen wheel the pairs are red and cyan, green and magenta, and blue and yellow. Both wheels are useful. One grew out of mixing paint and the other fits mixing light, as you saw in Lesson 1. When someone says two colors are complementary, it is fair to ask which wheel they mean. Artists also call the hues from red through yellow warm, and the hues from blue-green through blue-purple cool. People have used these words since at least the late 1700s, but they do not agree on exactly where the line falls. You may also hear that warm colors excite people and cool colors calm them. Researchers who reviewed the studies say it is too early to make strong claims like that. The rules for matching colors are guides. They are not laws, and tastes differ from person to person and from culture to culture. Use the wheel to find ideas, and then trust what you see.": "1009bf38d6b4",
